                        C h e s s w i z  1.0

=====================================================================

Website: https://chesswizapp.com

Chesswiz is a chess database application for Linux, Windows and macOS,
version 1.0. It is built on Scidb (by Gregor Cramer), itself inspired
by Scid, and keeps Scidb's fast native database engine (.sci) with a
Tcl/Tk interface.

What Chesswiz can do:

   - browsing and editing games in chess databases
   - database switching, filtered views, and working sets
   - database maintenance, duplicate detection, and opening statistics
   - material search and CQL/pattern-based search
   - player preparation reports from the players list
   - opponent dossiers with recent games, opening tendencies, and
     direct pivots into prep reports, switcher views, and working sets
   - correspondence chess: live clock display, auto-timestamped moves,
     ICCF XFCC server sync and auto-submit, game manager panel
     (see docs/features/CORRESPONDENCE_CHESS.md)
   - an AI assistant, analysis with bundled engines (Stockfish,
     Reckless, Sjeng), and Syzygy tablebase probing

For repeated search, maintenance, and preparation work, the native
database format (.sci) is the recommended format. PGN export uses
UTF-8 encoding by default.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
Build on Linux
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Requirements: a C++ compiler, Tcl/Tk 8.6 development files, and the
libraries listed in INSTALL (freetype, SM, Xcursor, fontconfig, gdbm,
zlib, expat, mpv, zzip). On Debian/Ubuntu/Mint:

   sudo apt-get install tcl8.6 tk8.6 tcl8.6-dev tk8.6-dev \
     libfreetype6-dev libsm-dev libxcursor-dev libfontconfig1-dev \
     libgdbm-dev libmpv-dev libzzip-dev

Then, from this directory:

   ./configure
   make

This builds the C++ interpreter in src/ and bundles the Tcl sources
into tcl/chesswiz. To run it straight from the build tree, without
installing:

   src/tkchesswiz -encoding utf-8 tcl/chesswiz

To install system-wide (use --prefix to install elsewhere):

   sudo make install

Windows and macOS builds are packaged by the scripts in scripts/
(see docs/dev/PORT_WINDOWS_MAC_SPEC.md).

